## The short version

Pick the **one idea** you're converging on and write a **Product Definition Brief**: a tight, ~2-page document that says exactly **who it's for, what they need, which needs matter most, and what "this works" would look like.** It's built from your **Discovery Notes** — real conversations plus your honest persona — not from your imagination.

This is the founder's core artifact: proof you understand a real user's need well enough to **speak for it.**

---

## Why this assignment exists

<course-link type="assignment" id="cp1">CP1</course-link> asked you to explore *widely* (three different problems). This assignment asks you to go *deep* on the one you're carrying forward. Founding a project means owning a problem on behalf of the people who have it — and you can't own what you can't clearly define. A vague target ("students," "everyone who's busy") sinks everything downstream: you can't scope an MVP, argue feasibility, or judge whether you succeeded.

The Product Definition Brief is the bridge between **discovery** (Weeks 1–2) and your **written proposal** (Week 4). It's a **lightweight product requirements document (PRD)** — the short, shared definition of *problem and user* that product teams everywhere write before they build anything. (For what a PRD contains and what it deliberately leaves out, see <course-link type="page" id="pg-prd-reference">PRD reference</course-link>.) Get it right and the proposal almost writes itself; get it fuzzy and every later artifact inherits the fog.

