## What to produce

A single document (or PDF), roughly **2 pages**, with these five sections:

**1. Target user.** Who is this for — sharply defined and *bounded*. State who's **in** and who's **out**. "Commuter students who park off-campus" beats "students." If there are two user types (e.g., an organizer and a participant), name both and say which is primary.

**2. The needs.** What these users are actually trying to get done, and what's in the way — drawn from your <course-link type="page" id="pg-discovery-notes">Discovery Notes</course-link>. State needs as **needs**, not features. For each, note where it came from: a real conversation (**[E]** evidence) or an assumption from your persona (**[A]**). Honesty about which is which is graded.

**3. Prioritized needs.** Rank the needs — **must-have vs. nice-to-have** — with a one-line rationale for the ordering. This ranking is what your MVP will later be scoped around, so it matters.

**4. Success criteria.** Specific, **observable** definitions of "this works." Not "users are happy" but "a user can reserve a practice room in under a minute without double-booking," or "an organizer fills a shift schedule without a spreadsheet." Aim for 3–5 criteria the final demo could actually be judged against.

**5. Open questions / what you'd still verify.** The biggest assumptions you haven't confirmed with a real user yet, and how you'd check them. (Founders name their unknowns; they don't hide them.)

---

## Ground it in real discovery

This brief must rest on your **<course-link type="page" id="pg-discovery-notes">Discovery Notes</course-link>** (due {{ S05_DATE }}): **1–3 real conversations** plus **one honest, evidence-tagged persona.** Tag claims as evidence **[E]** or assumption **[A]** the same way. A brief built only on assumptions can still pass — but only if it's *honest* about that and clear about what it would verify. A brief that dresses guesses up as facts scores low on the criterion that matters most here.

---

## Requirements & constraints

- **Individual work.** Weeks 1–5 are individual.
- **One idea.** This is about the single idea you're converging on — not all three CP1 briefs.
- **Length.** ~2 pages. Tight and specific beats long and vague.
- **Format.** Markdown or PDF. Plain and readable.
- **Evidence tags.** Mark needs and claims **[E]** / **[A]** so the source of each is visible.
- **AI use.** Fine for tightening prose or organizing; the user understanding and judgment must be yours, and you must be able to explain them. Disclose substantial AI use.

---

## Grading

Scored on a 100-point analytic rubric; **completes the 10% Discovery grade** (with CP1). Reach at least **Developing** on every criterion to stay on a passing track.

| Criterion | Weight | What it measures |
|---|---|---|
| **Target user clarity** | 20 | Userbase sharply defined and bounded — who's in and out is clear. Not "everyone." |
| **Needfinding quality & honesty** | 30 | Real interviews/observation **and/or** a rigorously documented persona — ideally interviews *informing* a persona — each with honest reasoning about its limits. **Honesty about method is graded as heavily as the findings.** |
| **Prioritized needs** | 20 | Needs prioritized (must-have vs. nice-to-have) with a defensible rationale. |
| **Success criteria** | 20 | Specific, observable, defensible criteria for "this works." |
| **PRD mapping** | 10 | Reads like a lightweight product-requirements doc (see the <course-link type="page" id="pg-prd-reference">PRD reference</course-link>) — a clear definition of problem and user, not a solution. |

**Performance levels:** Exemplary (4) · Proficient (3) · Developing (2) · Beginning (1) · Absent (0). Your score on each criterion = (level ÷ 4) × weight.

**The table above summarizes what each criterion measures. For the full four-level grid — exactly what Exemplary, Proficient, Developing, and Beginning look like on each criterion — see the <course-link type="page" id="pg-rubrics">Assignment Rubrics</course-link> page.** Read the levels before you write, not after you're graded.

